      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this branch leaves `*address` unchanged. For a 4KB VMA with `vm_ops` or `vm_file`, `vma_address()` goes through `vma_pgoff_to_address()`, which already subtracts `vm_slice_off` and clamps to `vm_start`, so it returns the first address of the folio\u0027s file page in the VMA. That address is at slice 0 or is `vm_start`, so `vma_folio_slice_bounds()` hands it back as is: with `vm_slice_off \u003d 1` and index `vm_pgoff + 1`, for example, both give `vm_start + 0x3000`.\n\nWould it be OK to drop this branch and the kernel-doc change, unless I\u0027m missing a case where `vma_address()` lands in the middle of the window?\n\nAlso, with the native-tree check in `p3s_anon_rmap_range()`, the lookup no longer finds native VMAs that hold no part of the folio, so this helper could return `false` for them and leave the `VM_BUG_ON_VMA()` in `rmap_walk_anon()` checking native addresses as upstream does:\n\n```\n\tif (!vma_is_p3s_4k(vma) || folio_test_large(folio))\n\t\treturn false;\n\tif (!vma-\u003evm_ops \u0026\u0026 !vma-\u003evm_file)\n\t\t*address \u003d vma_anon_slice_window(vma, folio_pgoff(folio));\n\treturn *address \u003d\u003d -EFAULT;\n```",
